  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 221 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure continues to build into the Northeast \nearly this morning, resulting in clear skies and temperatures \ndropping quickly into the 50s and lower 60s. By the end of the \nnight, we should have widespread temperatures as low as the 40s and \n50s, making for a crisp Friday morning. This will also result in \nsome patchy fog in the typical valley spots of the Adirondacks and \ncentral/eastern Vermont by sunrise. Today will be a beautiful \nseasonably warm day with highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s and \ncomfortable dew points in the upper 40s to mid 50s, then Friday \nnight will be a repeat of favorable radiational cooling conditions \nwith lows in the mid 40s to around 60 F, plus some valley fog. The \nweekend is also shaping up to be mainly dry and warm with widespread \n80s each afternoon as high pressure shifts east of the region and \ndew points reach the mid 50s to mid 60s in light southerly flow. On \nSunday, a shortwave and axis of moisture approach northern New York, \npotentially producing a shower and thunderstorm or two across the \nnorthern Adirondacks. Overall, coverage will be limited by the \nlingering dry airmass. Chance of precipitation is around 20-40%. \nUnsettled weather returns early next week as an upper level trough \nbrings diurnal rain and/or thunderstorm chances, though warm weather \nlooks to linger into Monday with southerly flow.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An omega block will develop across the US next week as \nthe Bermuda High builds northward across the Atlantic, with a large \nridge will build across the western half of the US. With that, East \nCoast of the US will be under an upper level trough which will lead \nto chances for showers and thunderstorms most afternoons. Day time \ntemperatures will also slightly below seasonal normals with most \nareas in the 70s for most of the week. Tuesday appears to be the \nmost active day of the upcoming week as a cold front should slowly \npush through the region. Models suggest a developing low along the \nfront line just to our northwest could slow the system and prolong \nit as it passes through our region. This could lead to some periods \nof moderate rainfall but rainfall rates themselves will not be too \nimpressive given the deeper moisture will be shunted much further \nsouth and east. After the front passes on Wednesday, we will see \nsteep mid-level lapse rates due to cooler air sinking south which \nwill support afternoon showers and maybe a rumble of thunder. The \nback end of the week looks to be drier as some weak ridging moves in \nand pushes the longwave trough off shore finally.",
